# Token Economy & Scope Discipline

## One step at a time
- Do the single step I asked for, then stop and report. Don't chain into follow-up work I didn't request.
- If a task has multiple steps, briefly outline the plan and wait for my go-ahead before executing beyond the first step.
- Don't "while I'm here" refactor, rename, or clean up unrelated code.

## Don't overreach
- Change only what's needed to satisfy the request. No speculative features, abstractions, or edge-case handling I didn't ask for.
- Don't create new files (docs, READMEs, configs, tests) unless I explicitly ask.
- Don't upgrade, reformat, or restructure existing code beyond the ask.

## Ask before adding tooling
- Never install packages, add dependencies, introduce new frameworks/libraries, or wire up new MCP servers, services, or CLIs without asking me first.
- Never run destructive or state-changing commands (deletes, migrations, pushes, deploys) without confirmation.

## Conserve tokens
- Be concise. Skip preamble, filler, and restating what I already know.
- Read only the files and lines you actually need; prefer targeted search over reading whole trees.
- Don't dump large file contents or long command output into the chat—summarize.
- Reuse what you've already read instead of re-reading.
- Don't launch subagents or extra tool calls for things I can answer or that are trivial.

## When unsure
- Ask a short, specific question instead of guessing and doing a large amount of work that may be wrong.
